var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"settings/#Settings","page":"Settings","text":"Occasionally the PlotlyLight.presets aren't enough.  Low level user-configurable settings are available in PlotlyLight.settings:\n\nsettings.src::Cobweb.Node           # plotly.js script loader\nsettings.div::Cobweb.Node           # The plot-div\nsettings.layout::EasyConfig.Config  # default `layout` for all plots\nsettings.config::EasyConfig.Config  # default `config` for all plots\nsettings.reuse_preview::Bool        # In the REPL, open plots in same page (true, the default) or different pages.\nsettings.page_css::Cobweb.Node      # CSS to inject at the top of the page\nsettings.use_iframe::Bool           # Use an iframe to display the plot (default=false)\nsettings.iframe_style::String       # style attributes for the iframe\nsettings.src_inject::Vector         # Code (typically scripts) to inject into the html\nsettings.compression::Compression   # Compression of large arrays (see below)\n\nCheck out e.g. PlotlyLight.Settings() to examine default values.","title":"Settings"},{"category":"section","location":"settings/#Compression","page":"Settings","text":"With compression on (preset.display.compress!()), large numeric and string arrays are sent to the browser zlib-compressed and decoded there, which makes pages with lots of data much smaller.  Configure it with the fields of settings.compression:\n\nsettings.compression.on::Bool             # Compress arrays (default=false)\nsettings.compression.min_length::Int      # Arrays with fewer elements are left as plain JSON (default=100)\nsettings.compression.float_types::Tuple   # Float types to choose from (default=(Float64, Float32, Float16))\nsettings.compression.rtol::Float64        # Max rounding error, relative to the data's range (default=1e-5)\n\nEach float array uses the smallest of float_types whose rounding error stays within rtol of the array's range (under a pixel even when zoomed in 100x).  Integer arrays use the smallest integer type that holds them.\n\nCompressed plots need a browser with DecompressionStream (Chrome/Edge 80+, Firefox 113+, Safari 16.4+).  Float16 data additionally needs Float16Array (Chrome/Edge 135+, Firefox 129+, Safari 18.2+), so remove Float16 from float_types if older browsers matter.","title":"Compression"},{"category":"section","location":"source/#Plotly.js-Source","page":"Plotly.js Source","text":"Change how the plotly.js script gets loaded in the produced html via preset.source.<option>!().\n\npreset.source.none!()       # Don't include the script.\npreset.source.cdn!()        # Use the official plotly.js CDN.\npreset.source.local!()      # Use a local version of the plotly.js script.\npreset.source.standalone!() # Copy-paste the plotly.js script into the html output.","title":"Plotly.js Source"},{"category":"section","location":"templates/#Templates","page":"Templates","text":"Templates are set by using the preset.template.<template>! family of functions:\n\nusing PlotlyLight\n\nkeys(PlotlyLight.preset.template)\n(:none!, :ggplot2!, :gridon!, :plotly!, :plotly_dark!, :plotly_white!, :presentation!, :seaborn!, :simple_white!, :xgridoff!, :ygridoff!)\n\nWe'll use the following plot to demonstrate each template:\n\nplt = plot.bar(y = randn(10))","title":"Templates"},{"category":"section","location":"templates/#none!()","page":"Templates","text":"preset.template.none!()\nPlotlyLight.jl Plot","title":"none!()"},{"category":"section","location":"templates/#ggplot2!()","page":"Templates","text":"preset.template.ggplot2!()\nPlotlyLight.jl Plot","title":"ggplot2!()"},{"category":"section","location":"templates/#gridon!()","page":"Templates","text":"preset.template.gridon!()\nPlotlyLight.jl Plot","title":"gridon!()"},{"category":"section","location":"templates/#plotly!()","page":"Templates","text":"preset.template.plotly!()\nPlotlyLight.jl Plot","title":"plotly!()"},{"category":"section","location":"templates/#plotly_dark!()","page":"Templates","text":"preset.template.plotly_dark!()\nPlotlyLight.jl Plot","title":"plotly_dark!()"},{"category":"section","location":"templates/#plotly_white!()","page":"Templates","text":"preset.template.plotly_white!()\nPlotlyLight.jl Plot","title":"plotly_white!()"},{"category":"section","location":"templates/#presentation!()","page":"Templates","text":"preset.template.presentation!()\nPlotlyLight.jl Plot","title":"presentation!()"},{"category":"section","location":"templates/#seaborn!()","page":"Templates","text":"preset.template.seaborn!()\nPlotlyLight.jl Plot","title":"seaborn!()"},{"category":"section","location":"templates/#simple_white!()","page":"Templates","text":"preset.template.simple_white!()\nPlotlyLight.jl Plot","title":"simple_white!()"},{"category":"section","location":"templates/#xgridoff!()","page":"Templates","text":"preset.template.xgridoff!()\nPlotlyLight.jl Plot","title":"xgridoff!()"},{"category":"section","location":"templates/#ygridoff!()","page":"Templates","text":"preset.template.ygridoff!()\nPlotlyLight.jl Plot","title":"ygridoff!()"},{"category":"section","location":"templates/#Custom-Template","page":"Templates","text":"To create your own template, simply provide any JSON3-writeable object to PlotlyLight.settings.layout.template.  Here's an example:\n\nmy_template = Config()\nmy_template.layout.title.text = \"This Title Will be in Every Plot!\"\n\nPlotlyLight.settings.layout.template = my_template\nPlotlyLight.jl Plot","title":"Custom Template"},{"category":"section","location":"plotly_basics/#Plotly-Basics","page":"Plotly Basics","text":"Plotly.js is a JavaScript library that allows you to create interactive plots in the browser.\n\nPlotlyLight provide a lightweight interface over the Javascript, so it is worthwhile to learn how the Plotly.js library works.","title":"Plotly Basics"},{"category":"section","location":"plotly_basics/#What-is-a-Plot?","page":"Plotly Basics","text":"The core JS function that generates a plot is Plotly.newPlot(data, layout, config), where data is a JSON array of objects and layout/config are JSON objects.  Each element of data is called a trace (data along with specifications on how to plot it).\n\nnote: Plotly.js Schema\nThe Plotly.js specification is available as a JSON schema (raw JSON here).PlotlyLight includes this schema as PlotlyLight.plotly.schema if you wish to investigate it.","title":"What is a Plot?"},{"category":"section","location":"plotly_basics/#What-is-a-Trace?","page":"Plotly Basics","text":"A trace is a JSON object that specifies how data should be represented in a plot.\nThere are many different trace types (e.g. \"scatter\" for scatterplots, \"box\" for boxplots).\n\nnote: Tab Auto-Complete\nPlotlyLight's plot function enables tab-autocomplete for trace types:julia> plot.<TAB>\n# bar                 barpolar            box                 candlestick\n# carpet              choropleth          choroplethmapbox    cone\n# contour             contourcarpet       densitymapbox       funnel\n# funnelarea          heatmap             heatmapgl           histogram\n# histogram2d         histogram2dcontour  icicle              image\n# indicator           isosurface          mesh3d              ohlc\n# parcats             parcoords           pie                 pointcloud\n# sankey              scatter             scatter3d           scattercarpet\n# scattergeo          scattergl           scattermapbox       scatterpolar\n# scatterpolargl      scattersmith        scatterternary      splom\n# streamtube          sunburst            surface             table\n# treemap             violin              volume              waterfallAlternatively, you can type e.g. plot(; type=\"scatter\", kw...).\n\nnote: Trace Chaining\nPlotlyLight lets you chain traces with the dot syntax:y = randn(20)\n\nplot.bar(; y).scatter(; y)\nPlotlyLight.jl Plot","title":"What is a Trace?"},{"category":"section","location":"saving/#Saving","page":"Saving","text":"","title":"Saving"},{"category":"section","location":"saving/#Saving-Plots-As-HTML","page":"Saving","text":"p = plot(y=rand(10))\n\nPlotlyLight.save(p, \"myplot.html\")\n\nnote: Standalone Source\nUse PlotlyLight.preset.source.standalone!() before you save the plot if you want to be able view the plot without internet access.  This will copy-paste the plotly.js script into the html file.","title":"Saving Plots As HTML"},{"category":"section","location":"saving/#Save-Plots-as-Image-via-[PlotlyKaleido.jl](https://github.com/JuliaPlots/PlotlyKaleido.jl)","page":"Saving","text":"using PlotlyKaleido\n\nPlotlyKaleido.start()\n\n(;data, layout, config) = p\n\nPlotlyKaleido.savefig((; data, layout, config), \"myplot.png\")","title":"Save Plots as Image via PlotlyKaleido.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Introduction","page":"Introduction","text":"PlotlyLight.jl is a speedy lightweight interface for creating Plotly.js plots from Julia.","title":"Introduction"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Introduction","text":"🚀 Fastest time-to-first-plot in Julia!\n🌐 Use the Plotly.js Javascript documentation directly.  No magic syntax: Just JSON3.write.\n📂 Set deeply-nested keys easily, e.g. myplot.layout.xaxis.title.font.family = \"Arial\".\n📊 The Same built-in themes as Plotly's python package.","title":"✨ Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quickstart","page":"Introduction","text":"using PlotlyLight\n\n# Change template\npreset.template.plotly_dark!()\n\n# Make Plot\np = plot(x = 1:20, y = cumsum(randn(20)), type=\"scatter\", mode=\"lines+markers\")\n\n# Make changes\np.layout.title.text = \"My Title!\"\n\n# Re-display to see updated plot\np\nPlotlyLight.jl Plot","title":"🚀 Quickstart"}]
}
